---
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import ToolPage from "@bt/ui/ToolPage.astro";
import AdSlot from "@bt/ui/AdSlot.astro";
import PdfCompressTool from "@bt/ui/PdfCompressTool.tsx";
import t from "../../i18n/en.json";
---
<Layout
  bleed
  lang="en"
  title="Compress PDF to 1 MB or 2 MB for Mexican portals, free | Baitaan"
  description="Shrink your PDF to 1 MB, 2 MB or any size a government, school or job portal asks for. It runs in your browser: your file is never uploaded."
>
  <ToolPage
    heading="Compress PDF to 1 MB"
    lead="Choose your PDF and the maximum size the portal allows. The tool shrinks it in your browser, without uploading it to any server."
    updated="Updated: November 2026"
  >
    <PdfCompressTool client:visible t={t.pdf} targets={[0.5, 1, 2, 5]} defaultTarget={1} locale="en-US" />

    <Fragment slot="guide">
      <h2>What it's for</h2>
      <p>Many portals limit file sizes: job applications, school enrolment, scholarships and online government services often ask for PDFs of 1 MB or 2 MB at most. Documents scanned with a phone are almost always bigger.</p>

      <h2>How it works</h2>
      <p>The tool turns each page into a good-quality JPG and builds a new PDF. It starts at the highest quality and lowers it step by step until the file fits the size you chose.</p>
      <p>It works best for scanned documents, ID photos and receipts. In a PDF with digital text, the text can no longer be selected after compressing.</p>

      <AdSlot unit="0000000000" label="Advertisement" />

      <h2>Tips</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>If a long file won't get under 1 MB, remove the pages that aren't required.</li>
        <li>Scan in black and white or greyscale when the document doesn't need colour.</li>
        <li>Open the compressed PDF before sending it: numbers and signatures should be easy to read.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Is it safe?</h2>
      <p>Yes. Your PDF is processed on your own device and is never sent to our servers. You can check: choose your file, switch off your internet, then press “Compress”. The tool still works.</p>

      <h2>Step by step</h2>
      <ol class="ms-5 list-decimal space-y-1">
        <li>Choose your PDF.</li>
        <li>Pick the size the portal asks for: 0.5, 1, 2 or 5 MB, or another.</li>
        <li>Press “Compress” and wait while each page is processed.</li>
        <li>Download the file and open it to check it reads well.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Why is my PDF still over the limit?</summary><p>It happens with long documents or pages full of photos. The tool gives you the smallest file it could make; remove the pages that aren't required, or split the document, and try again.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Will it still be readable?</summary><p>It keeps the best quality that fits the size you chose. Before sending, zoom in on numbers and signatures; if they look blurry, choose a bigger size or remove pages.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Does it work on my phone?</summary><p>Yes, in your phone's browser, with nothing to install.</p></details>
      <details class="bt-faq"><summary>Is my document stored anywhere?</summary><p>No. It is processed on your device and gone when you close the page.</p></details>
    </Fragment>
  </ToolPage>
</Layout>
